WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.720
35 years after the original "Top&nbsp;
Gun," Tom Cruise's Maverick has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.640
lot to teach the next generation of&nbsp;
pilots – but he also still has a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.760
to learn. From Goose's legacy to Iceman's&nbsp;
advice to the meaning of that final battle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:18.400
this is the ending of "Top Gun: Maverick"&nbsp;
explained. Warning: spoilers ahead.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:23.840
At several points during "Top Gun: Maverick,"&nbsp;
we see Maverick almost lose his wings within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.960
Navy forever. It happens at the beginning of the&nbsp;
film when he takes an unauthorized test flight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.400
it happens later when he dares to&nbsp;
challenge the restrictions of training,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.320
and it happens once more when he finally&nbsp;
loses his cover within the Navy with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:42.320
death of friend and former rival, Iceman — who&nbsp;
used his status as admiral to protect Maverick.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.560
This is, of course, the ultimate threat one&nbsp;
can level at him, as Maverick emphasizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.520
repeatedly that flying is all he knows how&nbsp;
to really do well. He spends the whole movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:55.840
fighting off the idea that he might be out&nbsp;
of the cockpit for good, until he's finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.400
proven himself enough to fly as team leader&nbsp;
on the mission at the heart of the film.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.800
"Good morning aviators – this&nbsp;
is your captain speaking."

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:11.040
A few hiccups aside, the mission is successful,&nbsp;
and Maverick returns home a hero. After that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.680
the film doesn't really tell us exactly what&nbsp;
he's going to do next in terms of his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.800
but we do see him back in his hangar, working on&nbsp;
that old P-51 Mustang he's apparently been fixing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.600
up for a long time. It's not definitive proof&nbsp;
that he's moved on, but watching Maverick take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:31.600
to the skies in the Mustang, rather than an F-18&nbsp;
or another experimental aircraft for the Navy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.600
suggests that he's begun making peace with&nbsp;
the idea that he doesn't have to be a naval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.400
aviator forever. By finishing the Mustang, he's&nbsp;
seemingly finished that chapter in his story.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.520
One of the key tensions in the film is the&nbsp;
relationship between Maverick and Bradley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:50.720
"Rooster" Bradshaw, the son of Maverick's former&nbsp;
navigator Goose, who was killed in action in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:55.840
original "Top Gun" film. In the wake of Goose's&nbsp;
death, Maverick did his best to step in and be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:00.880
a kind of surrogate father for his best friend's&nbsp;
son, but at some point that instinct went a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:06.000
too far. The film reveals that Rooster is still&nbsp;
angry over his father's early death, and also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.240
angry that Maverick pulled his application&nbsp;
paperwork from the Naval Academy, setting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:15.040
his whole career back by several years because&nbsp;
Maverick himself didn't think Rooster was ready.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:19.760
The film presents a journey for both men&nbsp;
not just as individuals, but as teammates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:24.720
as Maverick has to learn to stop handling Rooster&nbsp;
with kid gloves, and Rooster has to learn to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.200
holding a grudge. It takes a long time for them&nbsp;
to figure this out, but when they're shot down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:34.080
together behind enemy lines, they manage to&nbsp;
build what looks like a lasting bond. Rooster's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.400
presence in Maverick's home at the very end of&nbsp;
the film, and his pictures on Maverick's wall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.400
suggests that he'll be sticking around with&nbsp;
his surrogate dad for quite some time to come.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.080
Penny Benjamin is a character referenced in the&nbsp;
original "Top Gun" as one of Maverick's exes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.880
indicating that these two have a decades-long&nbsp;
history that Penny is eager to put behind her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:57.280
Throughout the film, even as Maverick is back&nbsp;
in her life, she repeatedly warns him from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.760
making further advances. Eventually, of course,&nbsp;
it's clear that something else is happening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.160
and when they get back together, Maverick&nbsp;
makes a promise: He won't leave again.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.040
He eventually keeps that promise by returning&nbsp;
from his near-death experience on the mission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.360
only to find that she's departed on a sailing&nbsp;
trip, presumably because she assumed he wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.640
be coming back. At the end of the film, it's Penny&nbsp;
who returns to Maverick, not the other way around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.000
suggesting that they've finally&nbsp;
managed to overcome their hesitancy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.160
Maverick's willingness to take her up in the&nbsp;
plane he's restored with his own two hands further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:33.040
cements the bond they've built. They're both ready&nbsp;
to settle down — and settle down with each other.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:38.480
When he's first introduced in "Top Gun: Maverick,"&nbsp;
Rooster is very much a foil for Maverick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:43.600
a painful reminder of what he's lost and the ways&nbsp;
in which he's failed in his life. But Rooster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:48.080
doesn't just exist to serve Maverick's character.&nbsp;
He's got struggles of his own to go through.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:52.960
In the training flights for the mission at hand,&nbsp;
we see Rooster emerge as the most cautious of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:57.360
pilots in the field, the one who'd rather&nbsp;
take his time and deal with known issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:01.920
than fly into the unknown or rush to action.&nbsp;
This prompts Maverick to constantly tell him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.320
to focus more on doing than on thinking.&nbsp;
It's hard for Rooster to make that shift,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:10.720
even as he sees his fellow pilots doing it&nbsp;
around him. Only when the mission is on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:15.040
line, and it's clear that he has no other&nbsp;
choice but to act quickly and decisively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.520
does he emerge from his shell to become the&nbsp;
kind of pilot Maverick always knew he could be.

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:23.760
By the end of the film, Rooster has emerged&nbsp;
as a more self-assured version of the pilot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:27.200
he was at the beginning of the film.&nbsp;
He's learned to let go of his fears,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:31.120
and he's also learned to let go of some&nbsp;
old pain, leaving him better for it.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:35.520
In the beginning of "Top Gun: Maverick,"&nbsp;
Maverick's been working as a test pilot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:41.040
for the Navy for years, spending his days pushing&nbsp;
the limits of new aircraft as far as they can go.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:45.360
But as we soon learn, the program where&nbsp;
he's made his home is about to be scrapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:50.080
in favor of unmanned aircraft, something&nbsp;
he's determined to prove he can stop.

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:53.360
Maverick fails to prove himself&nbsp;
better than an unmanned program,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:57.040
crashing the experimental jet and&nbsp;
leaving an Admiral very angry with him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:02.560
He's shipped off to Top Gun for one last mission,&nbsp;
but he proves himself there, establishing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:07.520
ongoing value of pilots in cockpits even in&nbsp;
an age when drones can do a lot of the work.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.600
"I have to admit I wasn't expecting an invitation&nbsp;
back." "They're called orders, Maverick."

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:18.080
We see Maverick's value to his mission&nbsp;
in the film, but one thing that remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:22.720
unaddressed is how the test pilot programs&nbsp;
will be affected by his performance. Will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:27.120
he be able to advocate to higher-ups in the&nbsp;
Navy for continued use of pilots like him?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:30.400
Can he save the jobs of the&nbsp;
people he used to work with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:36.080
or is his ability to move on from his old gig&nbsp;
a sign that everyone else has to move on too?

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:41.040
Though Maverick and Rooster make up the core&nbsp;
emotional narrative in "Top Gun: Maverick,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:45.680
they aren't the only pilots in the game. Maverick&nbsp;
works with several of the world's best fighter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:50.160
pilots throughout the execution of the mission,&nbsp;
and they all seem to have something to contribute.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:54.400
"What do they call you?" "Bob." "No,&nbsp;
your call sign." "Bob." "Literally?"

00:05:54.960 --> 00:06:00.320
Phoenix and her weapons systems officer Bob bring&nbsp;
a steadfastness and cool-headedness to the team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:05.600
while Payback and his own WSO Fanboy&nbsp;
bring a certain swagger to the team.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:10.640
In the final mission, they each have their role to&nbsp;
play, and by the end, they've all made it through.

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:14.000
It's clear based on the stakes of the mission&nbsp;
that each of these pilots will have their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:18.560
lives forever changed by being part of it.&nbsp;
They'll get promotions and commendations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:21.280
they'll take their valuable&nbsp;
experience on to other missions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:25.920
and they might even prove to be the next&nbsp;
generation of Top Gun heroes and instructors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:30.640
who'll teach the next class of great pilots&nbsp;
how to fly. Maverick's legacy is much more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:35.280
his own achievements. He's created a whole&nbsp;
new group of Mavericks to fly behind him.

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:41.920
In the original "Top Gun" film, Maverick faced off&nbsp;
against Iceman, a cocky, immensely self-confident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:48.320
pilot who antagonized his competitors and did his&nbsp;
best to get an edge over everyone, no matter what.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:51.520
"You wanna know who the&nbsp;
best is? That's him, Iceman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:55.840
That's the fly he flies, ice cold, no mistakes."

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:01.440
In "Top Gun: Maverick," that role falls to&nbsp;
Hangman, a hotshot young pilot who, when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:07.360
film begins, is the only active aviator of his&nbsp;
generation to have a confirmed kill in combat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:10.720
He brings a tremendous ego to the&nbsp;
training flights for the mission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:14.640
often showing an unwillingness to work&nbsp;
as part of a team, and as a result he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:18.880
left on the backup crew when the mission&nbsp;
finally flies out near the end of the film.

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:22.960
But Hangman is not content to stay on the&nbsp;
sidelines. He still has a part to play,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:27.200
and he finally shows it at the very end&nbsp;
of the battle, when he saves both Maverick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:32.160
and Rooster from an enemy jet after their&nbsp;
own hijacked F-14 runs out of ammunition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:37.840
He does it with his usual swagger, but also with a&nbsp;
demonstration that he knows when to be a hotshot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:42.400
and when to be a teammate. Hangman's got a&nbsp;
long way to go, but by the end of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:46.720
it's clear that he's willing to change if it means&nbsp;
being a part of something greater than himself.

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:51.200
We don't see a lot of Iceman in "Top&nbsp;
Gun: Maverick," but what the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:55.040
shows delivers some interesting context&nbsp;
about his place in the film's universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:59.920
and in Maverick's story. He went on to become an&nbsp;
admiral and the commander of the Pacific Fleet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:05.120
and yet he's still in Maverick's phone simply&nbsp;
as "Ice," implying the closeness they reached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:10.000
at the end of the original film has continued&nbsp;
in the intervening years. When Maverick visits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:15.040
him at his home and finds Iceman dying, the two&nbsp;
share an embrace and an intimate conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:18.880
suggesting that neither has forgotten&nbsp;
all that they've been through together.

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:23.920
When Iceman dies later in the film, it's a wake&nbsp;
up call to Maverick that he needs to prove he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:29.920
still has what it takes to be a leader and a&nbsp;
teacher, but it also feels like something larger.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:34.400
Iceman and Maverick represent two different&nbsp;
paths taken out of that original film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:37.680
one who became a leader and the&nbsp;
other who stayed in the pilot's seat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:42.800
determined to control the only thing he was&nbsp;
ever sure he was good at. Iceman's legacy within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:48.080
the film is a belief in the sustained power of&nbsp;
human pilots, but also a belief in the sustained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:52.560
power of learning to teach and help others,&nbsp;
something he finally taught Maverick in the end.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:56.480
The mission at the heart of "Top&nbsp;
Gun: Maverick" is a complex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:01.520
dangerous combat flight to destroy another&nbsp;
nation's new facility for enriching uranium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:04.560
before said uranium can be deposited at the site.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:10.400
It's a tricky ride full of surface-to-air missiles&nbsp;
and enemy planes, so the Top Gun crew has to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:15.760
their best to prepare for every facet of it. The&nbsp;
mission is, of course, ultimately successful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:20.080
the facility is destroyed, and several&nbsp;
enemy planes are shot down in the process.

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:25.200
This of course raises the question: What are&nbsp;
the geopolitical implications of this operation?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:30.480
It obviously happened under deep secrecy, to the&nbsp;
point that the enemy had no inkling it was coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:35.520
until a series of missile strikes launched to slow&nbsp;
their air response. The film doesn't tell us which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:40.400
country is responsible for the facility, or how&nbsp;
delicate U.S. relations are with them outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:44.800
of this particular issue. It's possible the&nbsp;
pilots at the heart of the mission may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:50.080
many more combat flights in their future, or it's&nbsp;
possible it'll all just go away because no one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:55.280
wants to admit to building the uranium facility.&nbsp;
Either way, it's fascinating to think about.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:00.640
In the original "Top Gun" film, it was understood&nbsp;
that these pilots and the institution which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:05.120
trained them would go on seemingly forever,&nbsp;
with no challenge to their necessity or their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:10.400
power within the U.S. military complex. By the&nbsp;
time of "Top Gun: Maverick," that's at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:15.280
somewhat in question. Maverick's test pilot&nbsp;
work is being undercut by drone programs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:20.320
and while that doesn't necessarily apply to&nbsp;
Top Gun itself, he also spends much of the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:25.360
butting heads with Cyclone, Top Gun's apparent&nbsp;
commander, who has little respect for Maverick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:31.840
himself and believes that his style of flying and&nbsp;
teaching is about to go the way of the dinosaurs.

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:35.200
"The end is inevitable, Maverick –&nbsp;
your kind is headed for extinction."

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:39.360
By the end of the film, of course,&nbsp;
Cyclone grudgingly grows to respect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:42.960
Maverick's unorthodox methods&nbsp;
and his abilities as both a pilot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:48.160
and as an instructor. He teaches by getting up&nbsp;
there and doing things no one else dares to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:52.880
and by ultimately proving that his way of doing&nbsp;
things is still valuable to the Navy. It's hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:57.600
to imagine a world in which this doesn't have an&nbsp;
impact on the immediate future of Top Gun, its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:02.240
students, and its instructors. Given how little&nbsp;
we know about Maverick's own work with the Navy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:07.040
after the events of the film, though, it's hard&nbsp;
to pin down exactly how that impact will land.

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:11.440
In the middle of "Top Gun: Maverick," when&nbsp;
Maverick goes to Iceman's house to talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:15.760
through his difficulties with the mission and&nbsp;
with training the pilots he's been assigned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:21.840
Maverick tells Iceman that being a pilot is&nbsp;
not "what I am," but "who I am." He emphasizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:25.680
that he's not a teacher, that he doesn't&nbsp;
know how to pass along what he's learned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:30.320
and that he can't handle the burden of what it&nbsp;
might mean if he fails. Iceman, communicating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:35.760
mostly through a computer because of his illness,&nbsp;
responds by typing "It's time to let go."

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:40.080
This means many things in the wider context of&nbsp;
the film. It means it's time for Maverick to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:45.360
let go of the guilt he feels for losing Goose and&nbsp;
for what he did to Rooster earlier in his career.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:48.640
It means it's time for him to let&nbsp;
go of the life he used to have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:53.280
and it means it's time for him to let go of&nbsp;
the fear that he's going to inadvertently cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:58.400
more loss of both lives and careers. Iceman&nbsp;
re-emphasizes this point more than once in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:03.040
the conversation, leaving Maverick with the&nbsp;
conclusion that he simply has to learn how.

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:06.720
By the end of the film, Maverick&nbsp;
has seemingly let all of it go.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:11.680
He's no longer afraid of losing who he is, or&nbsp;
what he does, or of letting down the people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:16.320
around. He's able to take his own advice,&nbsp;
stop thinking about it, and just do it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:20.960
and in the end that might be the most important&nbsp;
thematic throughline of "Top Gun: Maverick."

